WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
Saying it keeps heat in may describe the
outcome without explaining the science

00:00:02.526 --> 00:00:06.936
Expansion explains gaps in some
structures, sagging overhead wires,

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:08.824
and a liquid rising in a thermometer.

00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:13.465
In a bimetal strip, two joined
metals expand by different amounts.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:14.589
What follows?

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:16.462
The strip bends.

00:00:16.905 --> 00:00:19.970
The side that expands more
ends up on the outside of the

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.898
curve, which is the longer path.

00:00:22.595 --> 00:00:23.381
Exactly.

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:27.117
Real engineering also has other
ways to manage thermal stresses.

00:00:27.574 --> 00:00:31.756
For a liquid-in-glass thermometer, the
liquid expands along a narrow tube.

00:00:32.181 --> 00:00:35.513
Digital thermometers use a different
temperature-sensitive property.

00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:40.979
To read a laboratory thermometer, I let it
settle, keep the sensor in the substance

00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:44.564
rather than against the container,
and read the scale at eye level.

00:00:45.313 --> 00:00:45.698
Good.

00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:47.459
Following the teacher's instructions.

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:51.950
Infrared thermometers infer surface
temperature from radiation and

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.622
depend on surface properties.

00:00:53.978 --> 00:00:57.395
They do not magically measure
every object's inside temperature.

00:00:58.074 --> 00:01:01.901
Last puzzle: Sweat cools you
only if it can evaporate.

00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:06.107
How can liquid water become gas without
reaching its boiling temperature?

00:01:06.906 --> 00:01:09.853
Some particles at the surface
have enough energy to escape.

00:01:10.328 --> 00:01:14.064
Evaporation can happen below
boiling, while boiling makes vapor

00:01:14.064 --> 00:01:15.351
bubbles throughout the liquid.

00:01:16.098 --> 00:01:16.689
Yes.

00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:20.746
Escaping particles take energy
away, and energy can come from

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:22.278
your skin and surroundings.

00:01:22.611 --> 00:01:24.288
The remaining liquid can cool.

00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:29.531
Faster air movement removes humid air near
the surface and can speed evaporation.

00:01:30.343 --> 00:01:35.172
Higher temperature, larger exposed
surface, and lower humidity can also help.

00:01:35.787 --> 00:01:39.735
Humid air makes sweating less effective
because evaporation is harder.

00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:47.038
Rescue three bad answers: heat rises,
atoms grow when warmed, wool creates heat.

00:01:47.311 --> 00:01:47.737
Go.

00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:50.975
Warmer, less dense fluid can rise.

00:01:51.413 --> 00:01:55.480
Most materials expand because average
particle separation increases.

00:01:55.853 --> 00:01:59.206
Wool slows energy transfer
rather than creating energy.

00:01:59.882 --> 00:02:01.371
Full dramatic recovery.

00:02:01.644 --> 00:02:04.699
Always explain the mechanism,
use temperature and energy

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:08.201
correctly, and remember that
good insulation slows transfer.

00:02:08.519 --> 00:02:11.020
It never grants a hot drink eternal life.
